re of the angle $∠BHG$ is{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we trace along the letters of the angle name, from $B$ to $H$ to $G$, we can see the angle $∠BHG.$\n\n![](images/74/74313dcd58dc34b71abd45e99c0f5f72675ea48c9d63672f3b7425b41f5da1d8.png)\n\nTherefore, $∠BHG={80}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-027b2ab0-0683-5a73-bb01-0cf456888f61"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-027b2ab0-0683-5a73-bb01-0cf456888f61" :answer-field/id #uuid "027b2ab0-0683-5a73-bb01-0cf456888f61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1e680fce-b5b1-5897-8444-0c12746fc963" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1e680fce-b5b1-5897-8444-0c12746fc963"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e680fce-b5b1-5897-8444-0c12746fc963" :answer/id #uuid "1e680fce-b5b1-5897-8444-0c12746fc963" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-65326aa5-fc10-5d53-94e2-fb4acf89303e" :question/id #uuid "65326aa5-fc10-5d53-94e2-fb4acf89303e" :question/math-academy-id "q-154348" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a683321165b9b0a41f473cca01ded0a8bc42c9e51e3075d361ffa9166c363bf.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of the angle $\\overset{^}{PRM}?$" :question/worked-solution "If we trace along the letters of the angle name, from $P$ to $R$ to $M$, we can see the angle $\\overset{^}{PRM}.$\n\n![](images/5a/5a00a816c3d045bc40f26ca7321879df82c7fd56e0824d3a96a1ad2b00d9fa1b.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\overset{^}{PRM}={123}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1d4358bd-d077-583e-ac26-8109f9327679"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1d4358bd-d077-583e-ac26-8109f9327679" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4358bd-d077-583e-ac26-8109f9327679" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1360c185-666c-5773-ae8a-f14e10f87617" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6064bd67-0437-5533-8679-08152d2c3cb1" "answer-1360c185-666c-5773-ae8a-f14e10f87617" "answer-38d6075e-066c-56f9-b9e3-e9959c0124ab" "answer-acf8cbc0-1d1f-578c-afc0-9e7fd9bc5328" "answer-580b6383-de5c-5845-9509-06045591613e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6064bd67-0437-5533-8679-08152d2c3cb1" :answer/id #uuid "6064bd67-0437-5533-8679-08152d2c3cb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{113}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1360c185-666c-5773-ae8a-f14e10f87617" :answer/id #uuid "1360c185-666c-5773-ae8a-f14e10f87617" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{123}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38d6075e-066c-56f9-b9e3-e9959c0124ab" :answer/id #uuid "38d6075e-066c-56f9-b9e3-e9959c0124ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{57}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acf8cbc0-1d1f-578c-afc0-9e7fd9bc5328" :answer/id #uuid "acf8cbc0-1d1f-578c-afc0-9e7fd9bc5328" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{85}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-580b6383-de5c-5845-9509-06045591613e" :answer/id #uuid "580b6383-de5c-5845-9509-06045591613e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{95}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135018 :tutorial/content "An **angle** is formed whenever two rays meet at a point. \n\nThe common point shared by the rays is called the **vertex**, and the rays themselves are called the **sides** of the angle.\n\n![](images/ca/caa6fe6d949902d06c155f109fc2202bc4adbfc0c324591100a56337f6841279.png)\n\nConsider the following angle, made up of the rays $\\overrightarrow{{\\color{blue}{A}}B}$ and $\\overrightarrow{{\\color{blue}{A}}C}.$\n\n![](images/d4/d4a10fda5cbf508754812bb114e80de3f3aad529c54d6b82538284bda58cd737.png)\n\nThere are several different ways of labeling this angle.\n\n- One way to write this angle is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\angle C\\!{\\color{blue}{A}}B\n$$\n\n \nThe symbol $\\angle$ means \"angle.\" Notice that the vertex ${\\color{blue}{A}}$ is in the middle.\n\n- We can also swap the order in which we write the endpoints. For example,\n\n$$\n\\angle B{\\color{blue}{A}}C.\n$$\n\n \nNotice that the vertex ${\\color{blue}{A}}$ is still in the middle.\n\n- To save space, we sometimes write $\\angle {\\color{blue}{A}}.$\n\n- Finally, instead of using the $\\angle$ symbol, we can write $\\widehat{C{\\color{blue}{A}}B}$ or $\\widehat{B{\\color{blue}{A}}C}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135150 :tutorial/content "The **measure** of an angle is the amount of \"turn\" from one side of the angle to the other. \n\nWe measure angles in **degrees**, which have the symbol $^\\circ.$ \n\nLet's look at a few examples of angles with their measures.\n\n![](images/7c/7c8a1fb7268052f44c88734607cd8e38ae8b57b924ec9fb65a6cb8918c60266f.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The more \"turn\" there is, the larger the angle's measure.\n\n- A corner angle (like the angle $C$ above) has a measure of $90^\\circ.$ An angle with measure $90^\\circ$ is called a **right angle.**\n\nWe use the letter $m$ to represent the measure of the angle. For example,\n\n$$\nm \\angle C = 90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nWe'd read this as \"the measure of the angle $C$ is $90$ degrees.\"" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134032 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
